INT. GLASS-FOREST APIARY TOWER - DUSK

Hexagonal windows hum with ten thousand bees. WYNFRED, an old beekeeper in a veil of moth silk, pours smoke into a brass censer. SABLE, a young storm-witch with wet hair, sits on the hive-table. LUCAN, a knight with a cracked pauldron, stands by the stairs.

WYNFRED
You came a long way for honey.

LUCAN
I came for the queen.

SABLE
He means the hive's queen. Not the crown.

WYNFRED
Does he?

LUCAN
I mean what I said.

SABLE
You mean what you were told to say.

LUCAN
The king is dying.

WYNFRED
Kings do.

LUCAN
His physician says only royal jelly can hold the fever.

SABLE
And the physician is lying.

LUCAN
You don't know that.

SABLE
I know the smell of a man who wants a hive burned.

WYNFRED
Quiet, child. Let him say it.

LUCAN
I was told to take the queen. Alive if she'd come. Dead if not.

WYNFRED
And you believed it.

LUCAN
I believed the king.

SABLE
The king hasn't spoken in a month.

LUCAN
Then who sent me?

WYNFRED
Ask the hive.

The humming deepens. Wynfred sets down the censer.

LUCAN
I won't kill a queen of bees.

SABLE
Then what will you do?

LUCAN
I'll warn the king.

WYNFRED
He can't hear you.

LUCAN
(shouting)
Then I'll make the palace hear!

SABLE
Then I will!

LUCAN ^
Then I will!

WYNFRED
Both of you, stop.

LUCAN
He is my king.

SABLE
And this is my home.

WYNFRED
Then go together. Fire answers fire. Bees answer storm.
